#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hypergeometric_pfq

#% text
# `hypergeometric_pfq([a1, ..., ap], [b1, ..., bq], z)` gives the generalized hypergeometric function `pFq(a1, ..., ap; b1, ..., bq; z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The series is the sum of `(a1)_k*...*(ap)_k/((b1)_k*...*(bq)_k)*z^k/k!` (DLMF 16.2.1). It converges everywhere for `p <= q`, in the unit disc for `p = q + 1`, and only as an asymptotic series for `p > q + 1` unless it terminates.

#% item
# The functions `hypergeometric_0f1`, `hypergeometric_1f1` and `hypergeometric_2f1` are the cases with few parameters.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([1, 1], [2], 0.5)
#% output
#= 1.3862943611198906

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([1, 2, 3], [4, 5], 0.5)
#% output
#= 1.189874754256423

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([], [], 1.0)
#% output
#= 2.718281828459045

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# With two parameter lists of length 2 and 1 the function is the Gauss function:

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([-2, 1], [3], z)
#% output
#= 1 - (2/3)*z + (1/6)*z^2

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `0F0` is the exponential:

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([], [], 2.0) - exp(2.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# The 2F1 case:

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([0.5, 1.0], [2.0], 0.3) - hypergeometric_2f1(0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 0.3)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A 3F2 at `z = 1`, where the series converges since the parameter excess is positive, is not evaluated:

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq([1, 1, 1], [2, 2], 1.0)
#% output
#= hypergeometric_pfq([1, 1, 1], [2, 2], 1.0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hypergeometric_pfq_regularized`, `hypergeometric_2f1`, `hypergeometric_1f1`, `hypergeometric_0f1`
